  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nSouth Texas remains in a slight to moderate risk of excessive \nrainfall, leading to isolated to scattered flash flooding through \nearly Friday morning, then dropping one category level Friday \nthrough early Saturday morning. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms continue to develop across the Hill \nCountry along an outflow driven boundary south of San Antonio \nahead of the main cold front positioned over west-central Texas. \nPre-frontal shower and thunderstorm cells and streamers are now \nstarting to develop over South Texas, with plenty of instability \nto play with (SBCAPE > 3000 J/kg) and no cin. Deep warm cloud \nlayer of over 10 kft and moisture near the 99th percentile (PWAT ~\n2.5\") with low- level flow nearly parallel with the outflow, all \nfavor efficient warm rain processes and training. Coverage is \nexpected to increase the rest of today and continue through the \novernight hours as the boundary north makes its way through South \nTexas and interacts with the seabreeze, nearly stalling through \nthe night.\n\nScattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms will continue Friday \nas the cold front approaches South Texas and a closed mid-level low \nover West Texas aids in mid-level lift support and moisture \nadvection. The cold front will then push through Friday night into \nearly Saturday morning, extending into Deep South Texas and \nproducing a surface low just south of us over the western Gulf. The \nsurface low will usher moisture over the passed cold front, \nproviding overrunning showers and thunderstorms into the Coastal \nBend Saturday morning, moving inland over the Coastal Plains through \nthe day. We'll see a similar pattern but not as aggressive on \nSunday. The mid-level flow flattens out early next week, leading to \nlower rain chances Monday, then a lull in rain activity midweek.\n\nIn regards to rain amounts, I do expect greatest accumulations to be \nfocused over the Brush Country tonight through Friday, then the \ncoverage will shift its focus over the Coastal Bend into the inland \nCoastal Plains this weekend as the front weakens and positions to \nour south, aided by a surface low. Currently, rainfall accumulations \nthrough Saturday afternoon generally range 2-5\" west of I-35 and US-\n77, with 1-3\" east over the Victoria Crossroads and Coastal Bend. I \ndo expect there to be much higher local amounts, potentially \nreaching 10\", greatest threat over the eastern Brush Country \nincluding Duval, McMullen, Live Oak, and Jim Wells counties as well \nas eastern Webb County.",
